>> title: Greek Salad
>> category: Salads
>> tags: feta, tomato, cucumber, olives, oregano, no-cook
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: greek salad, horiatiki, horiatiki salata, village salad, choriatiki, greek village salad, tomato and feta salad
>> pairs-with: tzatziki, pita-bread
>> servings: 4
>> time: 35 min
>> slack: forgiving — the tomatoes only improve for the first hour in their own salt, and nothing here can overcook
>> step.1: salt 15 min, keep the juice
>> step.2: macerate 15 min
>> step.3: warm 3 min
>> step.4: cut in chunks
>> step.5: spoon over

Salt @ripe tomatoes{1%lb}(450 g; cut in big wedges) with @kosher salt{1/2%tsp} in a #colander{} set over a bowl for ~stand{15%min}, and keep what runs out. That liquid is the only dressing base this salad has — there is no vinegar in a real horiatiki, and the sharpness has to come from the tomatoes themselves.

Macerate @red onion{1/2%small}(sliced in thin half-moons) in @red wine vinegar{2%Tbs}(30 mL) for ~stand{15%min}. Fifteen minutes takes the raw heat off, and raw heat is the thing that makes people say they do not like onion in a salad.

Warm @extra-virgin olive oil{1/3%cup}(80 mL), @dried oregano{2%tsp} and @garlic{1%clove}(smashed) in a #small saucepan{} over the lowest heat for ~warm{3%min}, then pull it off before the garlic colours. Dried oregano in cold oil stays dusty on the tongue; three minutes at body-warm opens it, and that smell is what tells you this is not a tomato salad with cheese on it.

Cut @Persian cucumbers{2}(in half-moons), @green bell pepper{1}(in rings) and @kalamata olives{1/2%cup} into chunks the size of the tomato, and turn them through @&(~3)salted tomatoes{} and their juice with @&(~2)macerated onion{}.

Spoon @&(~2)oregano oil{} over @&(~1)vegetables{} and lay @feta{6%oz}(170 g; one whole slab, not crumbled) on top.

The slab is not a presentation choice: crumbled feta dissolves into the tomato water and salts the whole bowl, and a slab stays a slab you break into with a fork. This is also why nothing here is tossed — the layers are the dish, and the juice at the bottom is what the bread is for.
